  "copies": [
    {
      "headline": "Bowers and Nacua Bury the Assassin Early",
      "body": "Noah's whole case here is the backfield, and it's a legitimate one. De'Von Achane and James Cook give him 33.2 projected points at running back against a Bears-Rams committee that tops out at 26.3, and if either Miami or Buffalo game scripts positive, that gap widens. Drake Maye and Garrett Wilson keep him in shouting distance too. The problem is everywhere else. John is fielding two of the six best pass-catchers in football in Puka Nacua and Brock Bowers, plus Trey McBride playing flex, which turns his WR/TE/FLEX group into a 61-point machine against Noah's 48.7. Mike Evans in a new Kyle Shanahan offense is the wildcard that could close that gap, but he needs a monster week, not a good one.\nJohn's floor is just higher across the board, and he knows how to grind out matchups like this \u2014 a 10-4 finish last year and 6-3 all-time on his own brother says he doesn't fold under pressure, even against the guy with five playoff wins and a title from the 7 seed. Noah's the postseason assassin, but Week 1 isn't the postseason. Take the points and the tight ends."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Chase vs. Jeanty Decides Week 1",
      "body": "Kaleb's the worst-to-first Cinderella, but three straight 8-6 finishes since then say the glass slipper's been sitting in a drawer, and Isaac's still chasing the ghost of an 11-3 start that peaked in Week 3 of 2022. Neither guy needs reminding they're overdue, and Week 1 splits almost perfectly down the middle to keep both of them uncomfortable.\nThis one is Ashton Jeanty and Breece Hall against Ja'Marr Chase and Malik Nabers, full stop. Kaleb's backfield is a 36.5-point monster, Jeanty alone projected for 20, and Herbert gives him nearly a three-point edge at QB over Bo Nix. But Isaac's receiver room is the best in the building this week, Chase good for 20 by himself against a Cincinnati matchup that should feed him targets all day, Nabers right behind him. Hockenson quietly lags Fannin at tight end, and the kicker and defense edges Isaac holds barely move the needle. This comes down to whether Javonte Williams and Cam Skattebo can keep pace with Jeanty and Hall, and right now the math says no."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Grant's Receivers Try to Bury CJ's Backfield",
      "body": "Grant is Grant \u2014 nobody in this league knows which version shows up, the 3-11 disaster or the 9-5 pleasant surprise, and Week 1 gives no hints either way. What the projections do say is that his receiving corps is the whole ballgame here. Amon-Ra St. Brown, Zay Flowers and a Waddle flex stacked against a Denver secondary and whoever else is on the slate give Grant a 33-to-28.5 edge at wide receiver and another cushion at flex, where Waddle's 12.2 dwarfs TreVeyon Henderson's 10.1. That's basically the margin of victory right there before anyone throws a pass.\nCJ counters with the better quarterback, Joe Burrow over Baker Mayfield by two points, and a Steelers defense plus Ka'imi Fairbairn combo that nudges back another point-plus in the two spots nobody cares about. Jonathan Taylor and Quinshon Judkins keep the backfield a near dead heat with Omarion Hampton and Jadarian Price. Problem is CJ's been the league's answer to \"elite defense, forgettable everything else\" for four years running, one winning season to show for it, and this roster shape looks familiar. Grant's receivers are live, and that's usually enough."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Curse Guy Owns The Backfield Edge",
      "body": "Logan opens the defense of nothing, since he's never actually won anything worth defending, but the roster says he should be favored here anyway. Jalen Hurts over Jayden Daniels is a clean 4.3-point edge, and the backfield gap is where this really gets decided \u2014 Saquon Barkley and Jeremiyah Love combine for 33.8 against Kenneth Walker and Bucky Irving's 27.2, and neither Adam runner has the runway those two Eagles-adjacent backs are walking into. Add in a Chargers defense that projects better than Adam's Eagles unit and Logan's built a 12-point structural edge across three positions before anyone throws a pass.\nAdam's counter lives at receiver, where Jaxon Smith-Njigba and Chris Olave outscore Justin Jefferson and Tetairoa McMillan by a couple points, and Sam LaPorta gives him a tight end Logan simply doesn't have an answer for. That's a thin margin to erase a backfield deficit this size. Adam's the gold standard, 34-22 for a reason, never a losing season \u2014 but Logan's the guy who's led the projections plenty before and still found ways to lose in January. Week 1 doesn't care about January. The math likes Logan."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kittle's Bye Week Costs Christian The Opener",
      "body": "Christian built the sexier receiving corps and it shows \u2014 CeeDee Lamb and DeVonta Smith over A.J. Brown and George Pickens is a real 4.4-point edge, and Bhayshul Tuten in the flex gives him week-one juice most rosters don't have. But none of that matters when your tight end slot is a zero. George Kittle sitting on Christian's bench with a bye-week donut against Tucker Kraft's 10.5 is close to the whole gap right there, a full swing bigger than the receiver advantage he built. Add Lamar Jackson comfortably outscoring Trevor Lawrence and Peter's floor looks a lot sturdier than Christian's ceiling.\nFitting theater for these two \u2014 Christian is the walking boom-or-bust portfolio, tenth-place ceiling one year and cellar the next, while Peter's still chasing the version of himself that went 13-1 back in the founding season, before the collapse and the four straight years of not being that guy. Bijan Robinson and Lamar give Peter a real foundation this week; Christian needs Lamb and Etienne to go nuclear just to make the tight end hole survivable. Book says Basement of KK escapes by a possession, not much more."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Gibbs Is Too Much For Joey's Lunchbox",
      "body": "Leif shows up with a real top of the roster \u2014 Josh Allen edges Jaxson Dart in the quarterback slot, and Davante Adams gives him a floor no one on Mitchell's side quite matches. But this one gets decided in the mush of the roster, not the studs. Jahmyr Gibbs by himself nearly outscores Leif's whole running back room's margin, and Josh Jacobs mopping up work against a leaky Commanders-adjacent scheme in Green Bay gives Mitchell a two-headed monster that Derrick Henry and a banged-up-adjacent David Montgomery combo can't quite answer. Nico Collins and Tyler Warren tack on more separation at wide receiver and tight end, exactly the positions where Kyle Pitts still needs a permission slip to be relevant.\nThis is the matchup format working as designed: Leif wins two categories by a combined 1.3, Mitchell wins five by a combined 6.9. That is the whole projection. Fitting, too, given the parties involved \u2014 Joey Lunchbox has spent four years losing these close ones just often enough to stay lovable, while Mitchell keeps doing his 7-7 coin-flip routine and somehow cashing playoff wins anyway. Week 1 says the flip lands his way again."
    }
